<!--history-->
[
["One can't ever avoid the past, but no, retelling it is not my main focus,"]<VehVehLine| she explains. ["It's the magic itself that I'm interested in. I'm asking if it's possible to bring it to the present."]<VehVehLine|
<br>
(print: $stuff's ohNo)
](history|

<!--ethical-->
[
["No, from a //magical// perspective. The ethical arguments against such practices are a given; every child in this tower knows of these. I want to understand the magic itself."]<VehVehLine|
<br>
(print: $stuff's ohNo)
](ethical|

<!--anthropology-->
[
["Something like that, yes, though not so sociological. And just this one malificer. I want to know if what the Charlatan practiced was //only// superstition, or whether it could be replicated, like any science."]<VehVehLine|
<br>
(print: $stuff's ohNo)
](anthropology|

<!--polisci-->
[
["Of course not! I might spend too much of my time stargazing, but I'll never be loony enough for //political science//. 'Science', heh, it mocks the name. No, I'm interested in the actual //magical// science of the Charlatan's practices."]<VehVehLine|
<br>
(print: $stuff's ohNo)
](polisci|

<!--magicBrainwash-->
[
["Don't look at me like that. This is serious scholarship,"]<VehVehLine| she insists with reddening cheeks. ["I'm training to be an astrologian. My specialty is //forensic dinvination//. I - well, several researchers before me - have this theory: The reason that the Charlatan managed to seduce - rather, //brainwash// - the First Colony magi was due to an uncommon source of power. Something to do with the alignment of celestial bodies over the Askavan sky. Perhaps they were born under a special sign, or their spells only worked on certain days.
<br>
"Figuring this out is the first step to replicating the Charlatan's feat."]<VehVehLine|
<br>
Indeed, it seems that Veh-Veh has spent too much time looking at the sky, and she is walking straight off a cliff. It is the davehmars' policy to never discourage genuine curiosity. "Never nip a budding flower," so they say. Strange things do grow out in the frontier.
<br>
But you can imagine why her mentor - the principal of the Apesai school - has left young Veh-Veh to languish and wither by herself.
<br>
